---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const pageTitle = "Likutay Halachos - Traifos 3 Yoreh Deah";
const pageDescription = "Breslov teachings from Likutay Halachos: Traifos 3 Yoreh Deah";
---
<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="page-banner">
    <h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
    <p>Yoreh Daya 1 - Traifos Yoreh Deah</p>
  </div>
<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=EB+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,500&family=Cinzel:wght@400;600;700&family=Noto+Serif+Hebrew&display=swap');
  .lh-container { --ink:#1c1c2e;--parchment:#f7f3ea;--parchment2:#eee8d5;--gold:#a07830;--gold-light:#c8a050;--sapphire:#1e3a5f;--sapphire2:#2e5580;--sapphire-pale:#dde8f5;--ruby:#7a1a2e;--border:#b89860;--muted:#5a5040;--verse-color:#1e3a5f;--source-color:#888070; font-family:'EB Garamond',Georgia,serif; font-size:18px; line-height:1.85; color:var(--ink); background:var(--parchment); text-align:justify; max-width:800px; margin:0 auto; padding:40px 20px 80px; }
  .lh-container .book-title { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 50px; padding-bottom: 30px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .book-title h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.2em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--sapphire); letter-spacing: 0.03em; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .lh-container .section-header { text-align: center; margin: 50px 0 30px; padding: 15px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .halacha-header { text-align: center; margin: 55px 0 20px; padding: 20px 0; border-top: 2px solid var(--gold); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--gold); }
  .lh-container .sa-excerpt { text-align: center; margin: 20px 0 35px; padding: 15px 25px; background: var(--parchment2); border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); border-right: 3px solid var(--gold); font-style: italic; }
  .lh-container .ois { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15em; color: var(--gold); margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 10px; display: block; }
  .lh-container p { margin-bottom: 18px; text-indent: 1.5em; }
  .lh-container p:first-of-type, .lh-container .ois + p, .lh-container .sa-excerpt + p { text-indent: 0; }
  .lh-container .heb { font-style: italic; color: var(--ruby); }
  .page-banner { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a365d 0%, #2c5282 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem 2rem; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .page-banner h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: white; }
</style>
  <div class="lh-container">


<h1>Likutay Halachos — Yoreh De'ah, Volume I</h1>
<h2>Hilchos Traifos, Halachah 3</h2>

<p class="intro">
The Matter of the Eighteen <em>Traifos</em>
</p>

<p>
For Rabbeinu, of blessed memory, wrote in the discourse <em>"Hatzilah MeCherev Nafshi MiYad Kelev,"</em> etc. <span class="source">(Siman 50)</span>: that there are sweet waters <span class="gloss">[<em>mayin mesukin</em>]</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shemiras habris</span> <span class="gloss">[guarding the Covenant]</span>. And there are bitter waters, etc. — which is the flaw of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>. And then <em>"the dog descends to eat its offering,"</em> etc.; see there. And the eating is in the place of a <span class="key-term">korban</span> [offering], as our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="source">(Berachos 55a)</span>: <em>"The table atones like the <span class="key-term">mizbe'ach</span> [altar]."</em>
</p>

<p>
And therefore, [the animal] must be complete and free of all eighteen <span class="key-term">traifos</span>. For the eighteen <span class="key-term">traifos</span> are the aspect of the flaw of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, which is <span class="verse">"Tzadik Chai Almin"</span> <span class="gloss">[numerically <em>chai</em> = 18]</span>. And therefore it is stated regarding a <span class="key-term">traifah</span>: <span class="verse">"to the dog you shall cast it"</span> <span class="source">(Shemos 22:30)</span> — for then <em>"the dog descends,"</em> etc.
</p>

<p>
And with this is connected what Rabbeinu, of blessed memory, concluded there — the teaching of our Sages of blessed memory: <em>"This Torah student who sat in a fast"</em> <span class="gloss">[<em>hai bar bei rav d'yasiv b'sa'anisa</em> — the Gemara's teaching that a Torah student who fasts, a dog may consume his meal]</span>. At first glance this has no connection there. But in truth, the eating too is in the place of a <span class="key-term">korban</span>, and when one does not eat in a kosher manner as is fitting, G-d forbid, then <em>"the dog comes to eat,"</em> etc.
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the animal must be complete and free of all eighteen <span class="key-term">traifos</span>. And then the food ascends in holiness and is subsumed within the aspect of <span class="verse">"Tzadik Chai Almin,"</span> and then <em>"the lion descends and consumes its offering."</em>
</p>

<p>
And therefore, the essential <span class="key-term">tarfus</span> depends upon the <strong>lungs</strong>. For the lungs are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">shemiras habris</span>, which is the guarding of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> <span class="source">(as is brought in the words of Rabbeinu in another place)</span>. For through the lungs, the moisture and fat of the body are sustained, through which the essential sustaining of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> occurs. And through this, the essential sustaining of the marrow within the bones — which is the aspect of the above-mentioned "sweet waters" — the aspect of <span class="verse">"all my bones shall say"</span> <span class="source">(Tehillim 35:10)</span> <span class="source">(as is explained in the above-mentioned discourse)</span>.
</p>

<p>
And this is: <span class="verse">"And you shall be men of holiness to Me; and flesh torn in the field [<em>traifah</em>] you shall not eat,"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Shemos 22:30)</span>. For <span class="verse">"wherever you find a fence against <em>ervah</em> [sexual immorality], you find holiness"</span> <span class="source">(Vayikra Rabbah 24)</span>. For guarding against the eighteen <span class="key-term">traifos</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shemiras habris</span>, as mentioned above. And therefore, then one is called <span class="verse">"men of holiness,"</span> as mentioned above.
</p>

<div class="editorial-note">
<strong>Editorial Note:</strong> Traifos Halachah 4 is subsumed within Hilchos Shechitah, Halachah 4, Section 8. And Halachah 5 was lost, as is written above at the end of Hilchos Shechitah, Halachah 5 <span class="gloss">[referring to R' Nosson's note about the manuscript of Volume Kaf that was lost during the persecutions of 5595/1835]</span>.
</div>

<div class="translator-summary">
  <h3>Translator's Summary</h3>

  <p>
    <em>Traifos 3</em> is a short, unsectioned halachah based on <strong>Likutay Moharan I:50</strong> (<em>"Hatzilah MeCherev Nafshi MiYad Kelev"</em>). Its focus is the parallel between the eighteen <em>traifos</em> and <em>tikkun habris</em> / <em>Tzadik Chai Almin</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Key teaching: Sweet waters = <em>shemiras habris</em>; bitter waters = flaw of the <em>bris</em>. The table = the <em>mizbe'ach</em>. When the animal is complete (free of 18 <em>traifos</em>), the food ascends in holiness and the "lion descends" to consume it; when <em>traif</em>, the "dog descends." The lungs = <em>shemiras habris</em> = guarding the <em>da'as</em> = sustaining the marrow in the bones = the "sweet waters." <em>"Wherever you find a fence against <em>ervah</em>, you find holiness"</em> — guarding against <em>traifos</em> is <em>shemiras habris</em>, earning the title "men of holiness."
  </p>

  <p>
    The editorial note records that Traifos Halachah 4 was subsumed in Shechitah 4:8, and Halachah 5 was among the material lost in the 1835 persecution.
  </p>
</div>


  </div>
</Layout>